REGENT OF DHARMASRAYA

WEST SUMATRA PROVINCE

REGIONAL REGULATION OF DHARMASRAYA REGENCY

NUMBER 16 OF 2018

CONCERNING

WOMEN EMPOWERMENT AND CHILD PROTECTION

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

THE REGENT OF DHARMASRAYA,

Considering : a. that women and children have the right to obtain a sense of security and freedom from all forms of torture or treatment that degrades human dignity; b. that women and children are among vulnerable groups who tend to experience violence, therefore need to obtain optimal protection;

c. that the fulfillment of constitutional rights of women and children as well as the improvement of the quality of life of women and children, constitutes one of the obligatory affairs which is the duty, authority and responsibility of the Regional Government; d, that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b and letter c, it is necessary to stipulate a Regional Regulation concerning Women Empowerment and Child Protection;

In View of : 1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;

2. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4236) as amended several times most recently by Law Number 17 of 2016 concerning Stipulation of Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 1 of 2016 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection into Law (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2016 Number 237, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5946);

3. Law Number 38 of 2003 concerning the Establishment of Dharmasraya Regency, South Solok Regency and West Pasaman Regency in West Sumatra Province (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2003 Number 153, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4316);

4. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);

5. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014), as amended several times, most recently by Law Number 32 of 2004 concerning the Third Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014);

6. Regional Regulation of West Sumatra Province Number 5 of 2013 concerning Protection of Women and Children (Regional Gazette of West Sumatra Province of 2013 Number 5, Supplement to the Regional Gazette of West Sumatra Province Number 88);

With Mutual Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF DHARMASRAYA REGENCY and

THE REGENT OF DHARMASRAYA

HAS DECIDED:

To Stipulate : REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING WOMEN EMPOWERMENT AND CHILD PROTECTION

Article 25

The responsibility of parents and families to prevent acts of violence against women and children includes:

a. protecting women and children from various violations of rights within the household;
b. encouraging the formation of harmonious, educational and democratic families;
c. providing good examples to children in accordance with religious and customary teachings;
d. supervising children in their activities inside or outside the home; and
e. providing opportunities for children to engage in dialogue and express opinions democratically.

Article 24

The community's responsibility to prevent acts of violence against women and Children includes: a. monitoring various conditions occurring in their environment that may lead to violations of the rights of women and children;

b. increasing awareness of violations of the rights of women and children;

c. strengthening the role of community, religious organizations and the family environment; and

d. preventing unregistered marriages and marriage at child age.

Article 31

(1) Health rehabilitation as referred to in Article 26 paragraph (3) letter b constitutes efforts that include the aspects of: a. promotive, improving so that health status continues to increase;

b. preventive, preventing from contracting disease; c. curative, curing a person from illness physically and psychologically;

d. rehabilitative, maintaining so that a person who has not fully recovered can return to being fit as before.

(2) Health rehabilitation as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out comprehensively, accessible 24 (twenty-four) hours, of quality and carried out in accordance with health service standards.

(3) Women and children victims of acts of violence have the right to health services free of charge, including: a. medical services;

b. medicolegal services;

c. psychosocial counseling; and

d. Visum et Repertum.

(4) Further provisions regarding the procedures for implementing health services as referred to in paragraph (3) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 6

Every child has the right:

a. to live, grow, develop and participate reasonably in accordance with human dignity; b. to obtain protection from acts of discrimination; c. to obtain protection from economic exploitation and sexual exploitation;
d. to obtain protection from physical, psychological, sexual violence, and neglect;
e. to a name as personal identity and citizenship status;
f. to obtain a birth certificate free of charge; g. to embrace religion and worship according to the teachings of their religion with parental/guardian guidance; h. not to be forced to change religion by foster parents, adoptive parents and by anyone;
i. to be recognized and know their parents, to be raised and cared for by their own parents;
j. to obtain health services and social security in accordance with physical, mental, spiritual, and social needs. k. to obtain education and teaching in accordance with their interests and talents;
l. to obtain care, education, training, self-improvement and ability to participate in community and state life for children with physical, mental disabilities and special needs;
m. to express and have their opinion heard; and n. to rest and utilize leisure time for self-development.

Article 8

The obligations of the Regional Government in efforts to protect children include:

a. guaranteeing the human rights of every child without discrimination; b. providing support for facilities and infrastructure in the implementation of child protection;
c. guaranteeing the right of children to express opinions in accordance with the age and intelligence level of the child; d. establishing and implementing policies and programs/activities for the protection of children's rights; e. cooperating with the community and/or Community Organizations in the implementation of child protection in accordance with statutory regulations; f. conducting rehabilitation of children who are victims of violence;
g. providing special protection to children in emergency situations, children in conflict with the law, children victims of violence and human trafficking, children from minority and isolated groups, children exploited economically and/or sexually, children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic abuse and children with HIV/AIDS;
h. organizing inclusive education for children with special needs; and
i. taking preventive measures in order to prevent violations of children's rights.

Article 33

(1) Counseling services as referred to in Article 32 paragraph (2) letter a aim to:

a. help women and children victims of acts of violence recognize their problems and find effective ways to overcome them themselves;
b. empower women and children who experience criminal and/or civil problems to determine their future;
c. provide moral support to women and children who experience criminal and/or civil problems in facing the process they are going through; and
d. make women and children who experience criminal and/or civil problems feel accepted in the community environment.
(2) Counseling services are carried out by gender-responsive social rehabilitation officers, consisting of:
a. counselors;
b. psychologists; and/or
c. psychiatrists.
(3) Counseling services are carried out in a place that guarantees a sense of security, comfort and confidentiality of information from women victims of acts of violence.
(4) Stages of counseling services as referred to in paragraph (3) include:
a. initial counseling;
b. follow-up counseling;
c. case discussion; and
d. site visits.

Article 26

(1) In order to implement the Protection of Women and Children in the Region, the Regional Government establishes UPTD PPA in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

(2) UPTD PPA as referred to in paragraph (1) functions to provide services to women and children who experience criminal and/or civil problems.

(3) The scope of UPTD PPA services as referred to in paragraph (2) includes:

a. complaint services;
b. health rehabilitation;
c. social rehabilitation;
d. assistance;
e. law enforcement and legal aid; and f. repatriation and social reintegration.
(4) In carrying out its functions, UPTD PPA is obliged: a. to provide services and handling as quickly as possible to women and children who experience problems;
b. to provide ease, comfort, safety and free of charge to women and children who experience problems;
c. to maintain the confidentiality of women and children who experience problems;
d. to guarantee justice and legal certainty for women and children who experience problems.
(5) UPTD PPA is responsible to the Regional Apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the field of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection.

Article 59

(1) The Regional Government is obligated to provide comprehensive health services for Children, so that every Child obtains an optimal level of health from the womb.

(2) Comprehensive health services as referred to in paragraph (1) include:

a. promotive, preventive, curative and rehabilitative efforts for basic health services;
b. exemption from cost burden for malnourished children, children with disabilities, children with special needs, children infected with HIV/ AIDS, child laborers, child victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic and other addictive substance abuse, children who are victims of violence, and child victims of trafficking in persons;
(3) The Regional Government, community, families and parents must endeavor to ensure that Children who are born avoid diseases that threaten survival and/or cause disability.

