Article 52

(1) The Regional Government in the effort to fulfill the rights of Children is responsible for:

a. establishing and implementing policies and programs/activities for the fulfillment of the rights of Children;
b. respecting and guaranteeing the human rights of every Child without distinguishing ethnicity, religion, race, class, gender, ethnicity, culture and language, legal status of the Child, birth order of the Child, and physical and/or mental condition;
c. strengthening and developing institutions providing services for improving family quality in realizing the rights of Children;
d. guaranteeing the Protection, care, and welfare of Children by taking into account the rights and obligations of parents, guardians, or other persons who are legally responsible for the Child;
e. providing facilities and infrastructure in the implementation of the fulfillment of the rights of Children;
f. guaranteeing the right of Children to express opinions in accordance with the age and level of intelligence of the Child; g. conducting supervision and guidance in the framework of implementing the fulfillment of the rights of Children; and h. cooperating with the private sector and/or NGOs in the implementation of Child Protection; (2) The responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out by Regional Apparatus in accordance with their duties and functions.

Article 2

(1) The implementation of women's protection is based on the principles of:

a. best interests of women;
b. gender justice and gender equality; c. local wisdom; and
d. legal certainty.
(2) The implementation of child protection is based on the principles of: a. non-discrimination;
b. best interests of the child;
c. right to life, survival, and development;
d. respect for the views of the child; and e. legal certainty.

Article 23

(1) The Regional Government's responsibility to prevent acts of violence against women and children includes:

a. conducting socialization on the prevention of violence against women and children by optimizing the role of customary, cultural, religious institutions and community organizations;
b. providing guidelines on handling acts of violence against women and children; c. involving community participation in recovery and reintegration;
d. developing an integrated database and information system on acts of violence against women and children;
e. developing family empowerment programs;
f. strengthening the function of community organizations engaged in women's empowerment and child protection; and
g. providing comfortable and safe public facilities for women and children.
(2) The prevention of acts of violence against women and children as referred to in paragraph (1), is carried out by Regional Apparatus whose duties and functions are in the fields of: a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. education;
d. manpower;
e. women's empowerment and child protection; f. mental and spiritual;
g. informatics and communication; and h. peace and order.
(3) The prevention as referred to in paragraph (1), is carried out by Regional Apparatus in accordance with their respective fields of duty in an integrated and sustainable manner based on the Regional Action Plan.

Article 25

The responsibility of parents and families to prevent acts of violence against women and children includes:

a. protecting women and children from various violations of rights within the household;
b. encouraging the formation of harmonious, educational and democratic families;
c. providing good examples to children in accordance with religious and customary teachings;
d. supervising children in their activities inside or outside the home; and
e. providing opportunities for children to engage in dialogue and express opinions democratically.

Article 24

The community's responsibility to prevent acts of violence against women and Children includes: a. monitoring various conditions occurring in their environment that may lead to violations of the rights of women and children;

b. increasing awareness of violations of the rights of women and children;

c. strengthening the role of community, religious organizations and the family environment; and

d. preventing unregistered marriages and marriage at child age.

