Article 24

The community's responsibility to prevent acts of violence against women and Children includes: a. monitoring various conditions occurring in their environment that may lead to violations of the rights of women and children;

b. increasing awareness of violations of the rights of women and children;

c. strengthening the role of community, religious organizations and the family environment; and

d. preventing unregistered marriages and marriage at child age.

Article 3

Women's empowerment and child protection aim to: a. serve as a guide for regional governments in planning, policies and strategies for the protection of women and children;

b. protect women and children from actions, decisions and policies that violate the rights of women and children; c. prevent violations of the rights of women and children;

d. increase empowerment of women and children;

e. increase the role and value of local wisdom and the role of custom in the protection of women and children; andf. increase the role of government and non-government institutions in the region in efforts to protect women and children.

Article 59

(1) The Regional Government is obligated to provide comprehensive health services for Children, so that every Child obtains an optimal level of health from the womb.

(2) Comprehensive health services as referred to in paragraph (1) include:

a. promotive, preventive, curative and rehabilitative efforts for basic health services;
b. exemption from cost burden for malnourished children, children with disabilities, children with special needs, children infected with HIV/ AIDS, child laborers, child victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic and other addictive substance abuse, children who are victims of violence, and child victims of trafficking in persons;
(3) The Regional Government, community, families and parents must endeavor to ensure that Children who are born avoid diseases that threaten survival and/or cause disability.

Article 61

(1) Preventive efforts for basic health services as referred to in Article 59 paragraph (2) letter a are aimed at preventing the occurrence of disease or health problems.

(2) Efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out by:

a. requiring mothers to provide exclusive breastfeeding to babies until the age of 6 (six) months;
b. providing free immunization and vitamins to babies and toddlers;
c. providing nutritious food to toddlers from underprivileged families;
d. optimizing the role of posyandu for health services for mothers and toddlers;
e. conducting regular health examinations; f. providing sanitation facilities and clean water in every school; and
g. providing special breastfeeding facilities or breastfeeding corner rooms in government agencies or businesses.

Article 70

(1) In order to ensure the effectiveness of the implementation of the Child-Friendly Regency policy in the Region, a Child-Friendly Regency Task Force is established.

(2) The Child-Friendly Regency Task Force as referred to in paragraph (1) has the principal tasks of:

a. coordinating the implementation of Child-Friendly Regency policies and development;
b. conducting socialization, advocacy and communication of information and education on Child-Friendly Regency policies;
c. collecting baseline data for Child-Friendly Regency development; d. conducting needs analysis based on baseline data;
e. disseminating baseline data;
f. determining the focus and priority of programs in realizing a Child-Friendly Regency, adapted to regional potential;
g. preparing a 5 (five) year Child-Friendly City regional action plan and work mechanism; and h. conducting monitoring, evaluation and reporting at least once (1) a year.
(3) The establishment of the Child-Friendly Regency Task Force as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be determined by Regent's Decree.

