Article 6

Every child has the right:

a. to live, grow, develop and participate reasonably in accordance with human dignity; b. to obtain protection from acts of discrimination; c. to obtain protection from economic exploitation and sexual exploitation;
d. to obtain protection from physical, psychological, sexual violence, and neglect;
e. to a name as personal identity and citizenship status;
f. to obtain a birth certificate free of charge; g. to embrace religion and worship according to the teachings of their religion with parental/guardian guidance; h. not to be forced to change religion by foster parents, adoptive parents and by anyone;
i. to be recognized and know their parents, to be raised and cared for by their own parents;
j. to obtain health services and social security in accordance with physical, mental, spiritual, and social needs. k. to obtain education and teaching in accordance with their interests and talents;
l. to obtain care, education, training, self-improvement and ability to participate in community and state life for children with physical, mental disabilities and special needs;
m. to express and have their opinion heard; and n. to rest and utilize leisure time for self-development.

Article 8

The obligations of the Regional Government in efforts to protect children include:

a. guaranteeing the human rights of every child without discrimination; b. providing support for facilities and infrastructure in the implementation of child protection;
c. guaranteeing the right of children to express opinions in accordance with the age and intelligence level of the child; d. establishing and implementing policies and programs/activities for the protection of children's rights; e. cooperating with the community and/or Community Organizations in the implementation of child protection in accordance with statutory regulations; f. conducting rehabilitation of children who are victims of violence;
g. providing special protection to children in emergency situations, children in conflict with the law, children victims of violence and human trafficking, children from minority and isolated groups, children exploited economically and/or sexually, children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic abuse and children with HIV/AIDS;
h. organizing inclusive education for children with special needs; and
i. taking preventive measures in order to prevent violations of children's rights.

Article 25

The responsibility of parents and families to prevent acts of violence against women and children includes:

a. protecting women and children from various violations of rights within the household;
b. encouraging the formation of harmonious, educational and democratic families;
c. providing good examples to children in accordance with religious and customary teachings;
d. supervising children in their activities inside or outside the home; and
e. providing opportunities for children to engage in dialogue and express opinions democratically.

