f. to obtain a birth certificate free of charge; g. to embrace religion and worship according to the teachings of their religion with parental/guardian guidance; h. not to be forced to change religion by foster parents, adoptive parents and by anyone;
i. to be recognized and know their parents, to be raised and cared for by their own parents;
j. to obtain health services and social security in accordance with physical, mental, spiritual, and social needs. k. to obtain education and teaching in accordance with their interests and talents;
l. to obtain care, education, training, self-improvement and ability to participate in community and state life for children with physical, mental disabilities and special needs;
m. to express and have their opinion heard; and n. to rest and utilize leisure time for self-development.

Article 25

The responsibility of parents and families to prevent acts of violence against women and children includes:

a. protecting women and children from various violations of rights within the household;
b. encouraging the formation of harmonious, educational and democratic families;
c. providing good examples to children in accordance with religious and customary teachings;
d. supervising children in their activities inside or outside the home; and
e. providing opportunities for children to engage in dialogue and express opinions democratically.

Article 31

(1) Health rehabilitation as referred to in Article 26 paragraph (3) letter b constitutes efforts that include the aspects of: a. promotive, improving so that health status continues to increase;

b. preventive, preventing from contracting disease; c. curative, curing a person from illness physically and psychologically;

d. rehabilitative, maintaining so that a person who has not fully recovered can return to being fit as before.

(2) Health rehabilitation as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out comprehensively, accessible 24 (twenty-four) hours, of quality and carried out in accordance with health service standards.

(3) Women and children victims of acts of violence have the right to health services free of charge, including: a. medical services;

b. medicolegal services;

c. psychosocial counseling; and

d. Visum et Repertum.

(4) Further provisions regarding the procedures for implementing health services as referred to in paragraph (3) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 59

(1) The Regional Government is obligated to provide comprehensive health services for Children, so that every Child obtains an optimal level of health from the womb.

(2) Comprehensive health services as referred to in paragraph (1) include:

a. promotive, preventive, curative and rehabilitative efforts for basic health services;
b. exemption from cost burden for malnourished children, children with disabilities, children with special needs, children infected with HIV/ AIDS, child laborers, child victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic and other addictive substance abuse, children who are victims of violence, and child victims of trafficking in persons;
(3) The Regional Government, community, families and parents must endeavor to ensure that Children who are born avoid diseases that threaten survival and/or cause disability.

