Article 60

(1) Promotive efforts for basic health services as referred to in Article 59 paragraph (2) letter a are aimed at improving the level of health more optimally.

(2) Promotive efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out by providing socialization or education to the community regarding healthy lifestyles which include:

a. the importance of exclusive breastfeeding for babies;
b. the importance of immunization for toddlers;
c. clean living patterns;
d. the importance of nutritious food; and e. the dangers of drugs and smoking.
(3) In addition to promotive efforts as referred to in paragraph (2), the Regional Government may conduct other activities in the context of health provision.

Article 61

(1) Preventive efforts for basic health services as referred to in Article 59 paragraph (2) letter a are aimed at preventing the occurrence of disease or health problems.

(2) Efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out by:

a. requiring mothers to provide exclusive breastfeeding to babies until the age of 6 (six) months;
b. providing free immunization and vitamins to babies and toddlers;
c. providing nutritious food to toddlers from underprivileged families;
d. optimizing the role of posyandu for health services for mothers and toddlers;
e. conducting regular health examinations; f. providing sanitation facilities and clean water in every school; and
g. providing special breastfeeding facilities or breastfeeding corner rooms in government agencies or businesses.

