b. improvement of women's quality of life;
c. protection of women and children; d. improvement of family quality;
e. gender and child data system;
f. fulfillment of children's rights;
g. child-friendly regency; and
h. guidance and supervision.

Article 3

Women's empowerment and child protection aim to: a. serve as a guide for regional governments in planning, policies and strategies for the protection of women and children;

b. protect women and children from actions, decisions and policies that violate the rights of women and children; c. prevent violations of the rights of women and children;

d. increase empowerment of women and children;

e. increase the role and value of local wisdom and the role of custom in the protection of women and children; andf. increase the role of government and non-government institutions in the region in efforts to protect women and children.

Article 24

The community's responsibility to prevent acts of violence against women and Children includes: a. monitoring various conditions occurring in their environment that may lead to violations of the rights of women and children;

b. increasing awareness of violations of the rights of women and children;

c. strengthening the role of community, religious organizations and the family environment; and

d. preventing unregistered marriages and marriage at child age.

Article 61

(1) Preventive efforts for basic health services as referred to in Article 59 paragraph (2) letter a are aimed at preventing the occurrence of disease or health problems.

(2) Efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out by:

a. requiring mothers to provide exclusive breastfeeding to babies until the age of 6 (six) months;
b. providing free immunization and vitamins to babies and toddlers;
c. providing nutritious food to toddlers from underprivileged families;
d. optimizing the role of posyandu for health services for mothers and toddlers;
e. conducting regular health examinations; f. providing sanitation facilities and clean water in every school; and
g. providing special breastfeeding facilities or breastfeeding corner rooms in government agencies or businesses.

Article 25

The responsibility of parents and families to prevent acts of violence against women and children includes:

a. protecting women and children from various violations of rights within the household;
b. encouraging the formation of harmonious, educational and democratic families;
c. providing good examples to children in accordance with religious and customary teachings;
d. supervising children in their activities inside or outside the home; and
e. providing opportunities for children to engage in dialogue and express opinions democratically.

